member.summary:
The OIDC exchange of trusted publishing (tools/CONTRACT.md 3.1, design 13.4-13.7): validates a CI provider's OIDC token,
matches it against the app's trusted publisher rules and issues a 15 minute upload token. The token is never stored;
the claims of the last attempt are kept on the rule for diagnosis.

Entry point of the store engine for credential handling. Implementations talk to App Store Connect and Google Play;
tests use a fake. The secret is the JSON the user pasted: for Apple {"issuerId","keyId","privateKey"}, for
Google the service account key file as downloaded.

Reads the address of one app's icon, read only (App Store Connect: the icon asset of the newest build that has one;
Google Play: the icon image of the default language listing, read inside an edit that is deleted afterwards;
Microsoft Store: the largest square logo of the product in the public display catalog, read without credentials, so
 is not used). Returns null when the store has no icon for the app (for Microsoft Store
also when the product is not published), or without a call for a store that has no icons.

What a handler sees of its job and how it reports progress. Every step call renews the job's lease, publishes a live
event and, at most about once a second, checks whether a member cancelled the job (then it throws
).

The per app compliance timeline (docs/urun-plani.md 12.4): every catalog rule with a date (or a target API or SDK
minimum) that applies, or may apply, to the app, with its state. Assisted rules are done when their step is recorded;
target API and SDK rules are decided from the app profile; the rest is unknown until storeasy can check it.

A change plan before it is saved: what the planner computed from the store's current content and the desired values.
 names the content keys whose current values are fingerprinted ();
 names what is read again when the plan is approved.

Inserts or updates the saved answer document of  of an app. A non empty
 is sealed (envelope encryption bound to the app) and replaces the sealed one;
 removes it; otherwise the sealed password stays. Returns the row id.

HMAC-SHA256 webhook signatures. App Store Connect signs the raw body and sends x-apple-signature: hmacsha256=<hex>;
storeasy's own scheme (inbound signed webhooks and outgoing deliveries) signs {timestamp}.{body} and sends
X-Storeasy-Signature: sha256=<hex> with X-Storeasy-Timestamp (unix seconds). Comparisons are constant time.

Apple delivery:  uploads the IPA with the App Store Connect build upload resources;
 is the documented fallback where the CI uploads to Apple itself (EAS Submit, Xcode
Cloud, Transporter) and storeasy waits for the build in App Store Connect and applies the delivery rule.
